@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia - what happens when the transmission raises the counter-case itself
#
# One generalization, formed on 2026-10-04 from three attested claims of the
# rabbinic legal transmission, two of them read in this run and one standing
# since 2026-10-01.
#
# It is filed apart from pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation, which it
# resembles and is not. That statement is about a run that produced nothing and
# where the transmission sends it. This one is about a run that produced
# something the transmission would rather it had not, or an observation that
# contradicts a claim the transmission has just made, and about what the
# transmission does with the observation once it has itself put it into the
# record.
#
# The counter-search was carried against the compiled holding before the node
# was written, as SPEC section 16 requires. It did not refute the statement, it
# supplied the one case that bounds it, and it fixed the wording once.
#

pmc:TheObservationAnsweredByNarrowing a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The transmission raises the counter-observation and answers it by moving the claim"@en, "Die Ueberlieferung bringt die Gegenbeobachtung selbst und antwortet, indem sie die Behauptung verschiebt"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission states a claim about what has or has not power, and the same transmission then puts against it an observation of its own, introduced as something that is nevertheless seen, it answers by restricting what the claim asserts, by restricting what the claim is about, or by granting the observation and reassigning its cause; it does not withdraw the claim, and it names no observation that would be allowed to count against it. Each answer leaves the claim covering a class the objector cannot enumerate or a cause he cannot reach."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:HolinWhiteMuleClaimNarrowedTwice, pmc:AbodaZaraAfflictionsSwornToTheirHour, pmc:SanhedrinWhetherTheArtPrevailsAgainstTheDecree ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The three witnesses are three answers to the same form of objection and that is why they are held together. At Holin 7b the claim is that nobody who consulted R. Hanina about a wound from a white mule stayed alive; the discussion says that we see however that some stay alive, and the claim is read to mean, and is healed; it says that we see however that these heal too, and the claim is restricted to mules whose feet are white at the ends. At Aboda zara 55a the claim is that there is nothing in the idol; Zonin says that we see however that people go in broken and come back healed, and R. Aqiba grants the cure and assigns it to an appointment in time which the affliction was keeping anyway. At Sanhedrin 67b the claim is that there is none beside him even in a matter of sorcery; the objection is raised from the transmission's own saying that the practitioners defy the college above, and the answer is that it was different with R. Hanina, whose merits were great."@en ;
    skos:note "What distinguishes the three answers, and the statement names all three because the holding should be able to tell them apart. The first restricts the predicate - dying becomes not healing. The second restricts the subject - white mules become mules with white feet-ends. The third and the Aboda zara case leave the claim alone and work on the observation instead, one by an exception in the person it happened to and one by a cause that was going to operate regardless. A successor statement that collapses them will lose the only interesting thing here, which is that a transmission under pressure has more than one move and reaches for different ones on consecutive pages."@en ;
    skos:note "Why the objection being the transmission's own is the whole point, and the holding has been waiting for it. The same construction stands in the holding already, described from the outside, at pmc:BiansuiDispute: a first-century critic put a counter-example to a practitioner of the day-choosing arts three times and was answered three times with a fresh condition, ending at a clause that covers every remaining case. The note at that node says in as many words that a critic reporting the move is not a transmission supplying it, and the note at pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation repeats the reservation and declines to count it among its witnesses. The three witnesses here are the transmission supplying it, in its own canonical text, with the counter-observation raised by the editorial voice and not by an opponent, and in the Holin case twice within four lines. pmc:BiansuiDispute is therefore not a witness of this statement either, and it is now the outside description of something the holding can document from the inside."@en ;
    skos:note "The limit of the derivation, stated because it is the weakness a later reader would otherwise have to find. All three witnesses come from one transmission and from one of its works, the Babylonian Talmud, and two of the three from the same editorial layer that supplies the objections. What the three establish is therefore a property of the Babylonian discussion and not of transmissions in general, and the statement's reach is the reach of its witnesses. Widening it does not mean restating it more boldly: it means opening another corpus that argues with itself in the same manner and asking the one question above. The holding names three such places and has opened none of them - the Palestinian Talmud at these places, the commentarial apparatus of the Siku quanshu recensions that the Chinese divination file quotes at second hand, and the quaestio literature of the Latin schools."@en ;
    skos:note "What the counter-search found, and it is a bounding case rather than a refutation. Two pages away from one of the witnesses the same transmission does something the statement forbids: at pmc:HolinThreeTimesAgainstMosesOnce a comparative claim is grounded on a count of three dividings of a river, the discussion objects that perhaps it happened only once, and the claim is given up rather than narrowed. The objection there is about the arithmetic of the evidence and not about an observation that contradicts the claim, so it falls outside the antecedent as worded; but it stands as proof that the editorial voice which narrows is capable of conceding, and that the move recorded here is a habit and not a mechanism. The statement is left standing and the case is recorded, on the principle the holding follows at the fifteenth and twentieth witnesses of pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation: a claim is corrected where it is examined."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search over the compiled holding, in full, because a statement with three witnesses from one transmission owes its negative results plainly. Looking for a transmission that raises an observation against its own claim and then withdraws the claim: nothing outside the bounding case above. Looking for transmissions that raise a counter-observation at all, five came back and none of them falls inside the statement. The internal alchemy puts the practitioner's own complaint that he has held to the work and seen no result at the head of a chapter and answers it with two conditions in the operator, which is an empty run and belongs to pmc:NoFailedRunCountsAgainstTheOperation. The Zhen'gao reclassifies the one observation that would end the practice, the practitioner's death, as the attainment, which is a rule of reading imposed in advance and not an answer to an objection. The Visuddhimagga states that the absorption does not arise in spite of his practising and supplies ten remedies, which is again an empty run. The Zande oracle prescribes a second consultation whose contradiction voids the verdict, which is the opposite construction and the holding's best instance of an outcome allowed to count. And the Confessio excludes a class of patients in advance, before any observation has been made. The distinction that separates all five from the three witnesses is the same one: they provide for the case before it arises, and these answer it after somebody has put it."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search changed the wording once and the change is worth keeping. The statement first read that the transmission answers by restricting the claim, which the Aboda zara witness does not do: there the claim is left exactly as it was and the observation is given another cause. The third limb was added for it, and with it the statement stopped being about narrowing alone, which is why its own label is now narrower than its content. The label is left as it is because two of the three witnesses narrow and the file is named after them."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission of the kind described which puts against its own claim an observation introduced as something nevertheless seen, and then either withdraws the claim or names an observation that would count against it. One such case refutes it. The likeliest places to look are the transmissions in this holding whose texts carry a real internal check beside their claims, since a check is an observation already licensed to count: the Zande poison oracle with its corroborative second consultation, the forensic criteria of this same transmission at pmc:SanhedrinBloodAndDungAsTheTraceOfTheDeed, and the assays of the external alchemy. The second of these is the sharpest test available, because it would show one transmission doing both things, and it is the cheapest, because the pages are already read."@en .
